\name{as_miasim}
\alias{as_miasim}
\title{Shape the parameters for miaSim}
\usage{
as_miasim(x, x0, monod_constant, E = NULL, missing_rate = NULL, missing_resource = NULL, allow = character(), na = c("stop", "zero"), growth = c("measured", "phase_floor"))
}
\arguments{
\item{x}{CRM parameters from \code{\link{foodnet_listen}} or \code{\link{foodnet_crm}}.}
\item{x0}{Starting abundances of the taxa, one per taxon in the order of \code{x$taxa} (or named), each in the unit of that taxon's growth curve (\code{x$biomass_unit}); \code{x$biomass_start} is each taxon's own start. With your own \code{E}, they are passed as given.}
\item{monod_constant}{Monod constants in mM: one number for all, or a taxa by resources matrix.}
\item{E}{An efficiency matrix, by default \code{\link{crm_efficiency}} of \code{x}.}
\item{missing_rate}{A growth rate for the taxa that have none, or \code{NULL} to stop when any does.}
\item{missing_resource}{A starting concentration for the resources that have none, or \code{NULL} to stop.}
\item{allow}{Caveats to accept: \code{"mixed_media"}, \code{"stationary_phase"}.}
\item{na}{What \code{\link{crm_efficiency}} does with NA cells: \code{"stop"} (the default) or \code{"zero"}.}
\item{growth}{\code{"measured"} or \code{"phase_floor"}: the growth rates, as in \code{\link{crm_scale}}.}
}
\value{A list with \code{n_species}, \code{n_resources}, \code{names_species}, \code{names_resources}, \code{E}, \code{x0}, \code{resources}, \code{growth_rates}, \code{monod_constant} and \code{migration_p = 0}: miaSim adds random immigration even with \code{stochastic = FALSE} (miaSim 1.18's \code{perturb}), which in these units would swamp growth. Its other noise (drift, epochs, external events) is off unless \code{stochastic = TRUE}, and measurement noise unless \code{error_variance > 0}. To explore noise, change them in this list before the call, since it already holds \code{migration_p} (\code{args$migration_p <- 0.01; args$stochastic <- TRUE}); to turn it all off again, set \code{migration_p = 0, stochastic = FALSE, error_variance = 0}. Leave \code{norm = FALSE}: relative abundances cannot be turned back by \code{\link{crm_unscale}}.}
\description{
Returns the arguments \code{miaSim::simulateConsumerResource} takes: the numbers of taxa and resources and
their names, the efficiency matrix \code{E}, the initial resource concentrations, the growth rates, the
starting abundances and the Monod constants. With the default \code{E}, the starting abundances are put into
each taxon's simulation unit (\code{\link{crm_scale}}), and \code{\link{crm_unscale}} turns the simulated abundances back. miaSim is not needed to call this, and no other simulator
is assumed: the result is a plain list, so
\code{do.call(miaSim::simulateConsumerResource, c(as_miasim(crm, x0 = ..., monod_constant = ...), list(t_end = 48, t_store = 480)))}
runs it. miaSim stores \code{t_store} of its steps of 0.1 h, evenly spread; with \code{t_store = 10 * t_end} it
keeps every step and the run reaches \code{t_end} (fewer, and it stops early).

A simulation needs a growth rate for every taxon, a starting concentration for every resource, a
starting abundance for every taxon and Monod constants, so this stops when one is missing rather than
passing a number nobody measured: miaSim would otherwise draw starting abundances and Monod constants at
random. It also stops for parameters that pool every medium (their starting concentrations describe no
real medium) or come from after the end of exponential growth (the stationary phase), unless \code{allow}
names them.
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
args <- as_miasim(crm, x0 = crm$biomass_start, monod_constant = 1, missing_resource = 0, na = "zero")
set.seed(1)
tse <- do.call(miaSim::simulateConsumerResource, c(args, list(t_end = 48, t_store = 480)))
cells <- crm_unscale(crm, SummarizedExperiment::assay(tse))
}
